WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:02.305
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Welcome
to Get Cooking in Cloud,

00:00:02.305 --> 00:00:05.350
where we share the best recipes
to apply in your cloud kitchen.

00:00:05.350 --> 00:00:06.165
I am Priyanka.

00:00:06.165 --> 00:00:07.290
JENNY BROWN: And I'm Jenny.

00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:08.940
And in this episode,
we'll be making

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:10.727
a loaf of gluten-free bread.

00:00:10.727 --> 00:00:12.060
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Yes, we will.

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:14.130
There's a lot going
on in this recipe

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:15.570
that we will need
to pay attention

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:18.570
to-- keeping ingredients
separate from the allergens,

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:20.850
keeping ingredients
separate from each other,

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:23.168
and making sure our
space is allergen-free.

00:00:23.168 --> 00:00:24.960
JENNY BROWN: Much like
with cloud security,

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:27.280
we need to keep things
safe from external threats,

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:29.740
and even from mixing internally
before it's appropriate.

00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:33.620
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:39.187 --> 00:00:41.270
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Dinner
Winner is an application

00:00:41.270 --> 00:00:43.430
that collects recipe
submissions from users

00:00:43.430 --> 00:00:45.650
all over the world and
posts regular winning

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:47.432
recipes for everyone to enjoy.

00:00:47.432 --> 00:00:48.890
JENNY BROWN: The
recipe submissions

00:00:48.890 --> 00:00:50.600
need to be assessed
for clarity and then

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:52.850
scrubbed for any
identifiable information

00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:54.423
before they are
sent on to judging.

00:00:54.423 --> 00:00:56.090
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
The anonymous judging

00:00:56.090 --> 00:00:58.850
takes place, after which
winners are contacted

00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:01.307
and their recipes are
posted to the application.

00:01:01.307 --> 00:01:03.890
JENNY BROWN: Submitted recipes
can't mix with reviewed recipes

00:01:03.890 --> 00:01:06.560
due to the potential private
information from contestants.

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:09.110
So it's critical to
uphold that separation.

00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:11.150
Just like with creating
gluten-free recipes,

00:01:11.150 --> 00:01:13.970
it's important to not only
have gluten-free ingredients,

00:01:13.970 --> 00:01:15.935
but a gluten-free space as well.

00:01:15.935 --> 00:01:17.060
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Exactly.

00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:20.640
Our recipe is simple, but
this situation is complicated.

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:23.570
And for Dinner Winner, due to
the high number of submissions,

00:01:23.570 --> 00:01:25.280
that's a lot to handle.

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:27.470
JENNY BROWN: Do we need a
dedicated facility or just

00:01:27.470 --> 00:01:28.760
a strict cleaning policy?

00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:30.260
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Well, it depends.

00:01:30.260 --> 00:01:32.450
Let's see how their
system works today.

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:35.780
During the competition times,
users upload their recipe

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:37.100
in the text format.

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:39.740
These recipes get
stored in blob storage

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:41.390
from where they're
pretty manually

00:01:41.390 --> 00:01:44.180
checked for private information
like emails and phone

00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:46.190
numbers of participants.

00:01:46.190 --> 00:01:48.470
This private information
is then removed

00:01:48.470 --> 00:01:51.440
before sending to
the judges to vote.

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:54.410
The winning recipe then makes
it on the platform for everyone

00:01:54.410 --> 00:01:55.415
to see.

00:01:55.415 --> 00:01:56.790
JENNY BROWN: The
problem with it?

00:01:56.790 --> 00:01:59.480
Well, there's a lot of
problems with this arrangement.

00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:03.200
One, because this is a manual
process with a ton of volume,

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:04.850
this system will scale badly.

00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:07.160
But the biggest problem
here is security.

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:08.990
It's difficult to know
what's been reviewed

00:02:08.990 --> 00:02:10.639
and what's been
updated and what still

00:02:10.639 --> 00:02:12.122
has private information in it.

00:02:12.122 --> 00:02:14.330
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: What if
just one recipe submission

00:02:14.330 --> 00:02:17.420
gets accidentally shared with
the judges with a contestant's

00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:19.500
name and contact information?

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:21.590
This will potentially
make the contest unfair

00:02:21.590 --> 00:02:24.380
and can lead to brand damage
for Dinner Winner, which

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:26.492
sounds like a losing situation.

00:02:26.492 --> 00:02:27.950
JENNY BROWN: And
since Dinner Loser

00:02:27.950 --> 00:02:29.728
doesn't have the
same ring to it,

00:02:29.728 --> 00:02:31.520
let's make sure they
can keep their recipes

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:33.890
clean of personal information
just as we need to keep

00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:35.248
this surface clean of gluten.

00:02:35.248 --> 00:02:37.040
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Just
like Dinner Winner,

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:39.920
in every organization,
data protection officers

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:43.130
like you face an ever
increasing amount of data,

00:02:43.130 --> 00:02:46.580
data that must be protected
and treated appropriately.

00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:49.190
Quarantining and
classifying that data

00:02:49.190 --> 00:02:51.920
can be complicated and
time consuming, especially

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:54.907
given hundreds or
thousands of files a day.

00:02:54.907 --> 00:02:56.990
JENNY BROWN: And what if
you could take each file,

00:02:56.990 --> 00:02:58.790
upload it to a
quarantine location,

00:02:58.790 --> 00:03:00.590
and have it
automatically classified

00:03:00.590 --> 00:03:03.020
and then moved to the
appropriate location

00:03:03.020 --> 00:03:04.917
based on the
classification result?

00:03:04.917 --> 00:03:06.750
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: In
the next few episodes,

00:03:06.750 --> 00:03:09.260
we will show you how to
implement such a system

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:12.740
by using Cloud Functions, Cloud
Storage, and the Cloud Data

00:03:12.740 --> 00:03:14.072
Loss Prevention API.

00:03:14.072 --> 00:03:16.280
JENNY BROWN: Dinner Winner
needs an automated process

00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:18.830
for sorting submissions
and categorizing reviewed

00:03:18.830 --> 00:03:19.550
submissions.

00:03:19.550 --> 00:03:21.008
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
The ingredients?

00:03:21.008 --> 00:03:24.230
App Engine for front end, Google
Cloud Storage to store recipe

00:03:24.230 --> 00:03:27.950
submission files, Pub/Sub for
messaging, Cloud Functions

00:03:27.950 --> 00:03:30.680
for some quick automation
in serverless fashion--

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:32.750
JENNY BROWN: And the most
important ingredient--

00:03:32.750 --> 00:03:35.137
DLP API to detect
private information.

00:03:35.137 --> 00:03:37.220
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Now
let's put this all together

00:03:37.220 --> 00:03:39.140
and stir it up.

00:03:39.140 --> 00:03:41.720
Let's say the front end
is an app engine where

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:43.550
the user submits the recipe.

00:03:43.550 --> 00:03:45.540
The user would
upload the text file,

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:47.500
including the recipe
and info types,

00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:50.690
like the name, date, email,
and phone numbers in the web

00:03:50.690 --> 00:03:51.530
interface.

00:03:51.530 --> 00:03:54.680
The uploaded file is sent
to Google Cloud Storage.

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:57.710
This triggers a Cloud Function
to create the data loss

00:03:57.710 --> 00:03:59.930
prevention job in Pub/Sub.

00:03:59.930 --> 00:04:02.120
JENNY BROWN: Once the event
is received by Pub/Sub,

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:03.770
it triggers another
Cloud Function,

00:04:03.770 --> 00:04:06.020
which calls the
DLP API and looks

00:04:06.020 --> 00:04:09.140
for the confidential
information in the recipe files.

00:04:09.140 --> 00:04:12.890
Once found, it moves the file
to a separate storage bucket.

00:04:12.890 --> 00:04:15.950
If the file does not contain
any confidential information,

00:04:15.950 --> 00:04:19.220
like the info types email,
address, phone number,

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:21.920
then the function moves
it to another bucket.

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:24.710
This request flow keeps the
confidential data separate

00:04:24.710 --> 00:04:27.560
from the non-confidential
and is fairly automated,

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:31.070
such that as soon as the file
hits the Cloud Storage bucket,

00:04:31.070 --> 00:04:33.350
all other processes
kick off automatically

00:04:33.350 --> 00:04:34.342
to protect the data.

00:04:34.342 --> 00:04:36.050
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Now that we understand

00:04:36.050 --> 00:04:38.210
the problem and the
use case, we know

00:04:38.210 --> 00:04:41.005
exactly what needs to be done
to implement the solution.

00:04:41.005 --> 00:04:42.380
JENNY BROWN: And
next week, we'll

00:04:42.380 --> 00:04:45.030
reveal our step-by-step
plan for Dinner Winner.

00:04:45.030 --> 00:04:48.122
And maybe we'll even get
to that gluten-free bread.

00:04:48.122 --> 00:04:49.580
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Yeah, but first,

00:04:49.580 --> 00:04:51.800
we will need to avoid
cross-contamination

00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:53.213
by deep cleaning the kitchen.

00:04:53.213 --> 00:04:55.130
JENNY BROWN: And since
Get Cleaning with Cloud

00:04:55.130 --> 00:04:56.660
isn't quite what
we're going for,

00:04:56.660 --> 00:04:58.493
we've got some behind
the scenes work to do.

00:04:58.493 --> 00:05:00.035
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
While we're at it,

00:05:00.035 --> 00:05:01.800
don't forget to give
us a Like, comment,

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:04.880
and subscribe to learn what
you can cook up with the Cloud.

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:08.530
[MUSIC PLAYING]

